ial Medal of Freedom\",\"Presidential transition of John F. Kennedy\",\"Presidents of the United States on U.S. postage stamps\",\"Prevention of an Arms Race in Outer Space\",\"Profile in Courage Award\",\"Profiles in Courage\",\"Project Azorian\",\"Project Coldfeet\",\"Project Dark Gene\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Project Genetrix\",\"Project Grab Bag\",\"Project HOMERUN\",\"Project Hula\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Project Moby Dick\",\"Project Mogul\",\"Pushinka\",\"Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty\",\"Reactions to the assassination of John F. Kennedy\",\"Reagan Doctrine\",\"Red Scare\",\"Red Wave\",\"Refusenik\",\"Remarks at Amherst College on the Arts\",\"Repetition (rhetorical device)\",\"Report to the American People on Civil Rights\",\"Resolute desk\",\"Revenue Act of 1962\",\"Reykjavík Summit\",\"Robert F. Kennedy\",\"Robert McNamara\",\"Rollback\",\"Rose Kennedy\",\"Rose Schlossberg\",\"Rosemary Kennedy\",\"Roswell Garst\",\"Runnymede\",\"Russian-American Industrial Corporation\",\"Russian Embassy School in Washington, D.C.\",\"Russian Empire–United States relations\",\"Russian Famine Relief Act\",\"Russian Life\",\"Russian Soviet Government Bureau\",\"Russian War Relief\",\"Russian ambassador's residence in Washington, D.C.\",\"Russia–United States relations\",\"S/Y Manitou\",\"SS-100-X\",\"SS Dakotan\",\"SS Indigirka\",\"SS Iowan\",\"SS Metallurg Anosov\",\"START I\",\"Samantha Smith\",\"Sardar (horse)\",\"Seaboard World Airlines Flight 253A\",\"Self-propelled barge T-36\",\"Seven Days to the River Rhine\",\"Sheldon names\",\"Shoe-banging incident\",\"Shostakovich v. Twentieth Century-Fox Film Corp.\",\"Shvetsov M-25\",\"Siberian Seven\",\"Siberian intervention\",\"Sisson Documents\",\"Situation Room\",\"Smolensk Archive\",\"Society for Technical Aid to Soviet Russia\",\"Sovereignty of Puerto Rico during the Cold War\",\"Sovfoto\",\"Soviet Government Purchasing Commission in the U.S.\",\"Soviet Interview Project\",\"Soviet Military Power\",\"Soviet Nuclear Threat Reduction Act of 1991\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Soviet Union–United States relations\",\"Soviet espionage in the United States\",\"Soviet submarine K-129 (1960)\",\"Soviet submarine K-219\",\"Soviet–American Gallium Experiment\",\"Space Race\",\"Space policy of the United States\",\"Spaso House\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Stalin Note\",\"Stalingrad (1990 film)\",\"Stand in the Schoolhouse Door\",\"State funeral of John F. Kennedy\",\"State of the Union\",\"State visit by Nikita Khrushchev to the United States\",\"Statue of John F. Kennedy (Boston)\",\"Stimson Doctrine\",\"Strategic Arms Limitation Talks\",\"Strategic Defense Initiative\",\"Student Struggle for Soviet Jewry\",\"Super Series\",\"Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program\",\"Suzanne Massie\",\"TOPAZ nuclear reactor\",\"Tatiana Schlossberg\",\"Tear down this wall!\",\"Ted Kennedy\",\"Ted Sorensen\",\"Tehran Conference\",\"The Admiral's Daughter\",\"The Extraordinary Adventures of Mr. West in the Land of the Bolsheviks\",\"The Moscow rules\",\"Threshold Test Ban Treaty\",\"Timeline of the Cold War\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"American University speech\"},\"feedback\":1.365700387292557},{\"t\":3,\"state\":{\"currentTitle\":\"American University speech\",\"summary\":\"The American University speech, titled \\\"A Strategy of Peace\\\", was a commencement address delivered by United States President John F. Kennedy at the American University in Washington, D.C., on Monday, June 10, 1963. Widely considered one of the most powerful speeches Kennedy delivered, he not only outlined a plan to curb nuclear arms, but also \\\"laid out a hopeful, yet realistic route for world peace at a time when the U.S. and Soviet Union faced the potential for an escalating nuclear arms race.\\\" In the speech, Kennedy announced his agreement to negotiations \\\"toward early agreement on a comprehensive test ban treaty\\\" (which resulted in the Nuclear Test-Ban Treaty) and also announced, for the purpose of showing \\\"good faith and solemn convictions\\\", his decision to unilaterally suspend all U.S. atmospheric testing of nuclear weapons as long as all other nations would do the same. Noteworthy are his comments that the United States was seeking a goal of \\\"complete disarmament\\\" of nuclear weapons and his vow that America \\\"will never start a war\\\". \\nThe speech was unusual in its peaceful outreach to the Soviet Union at the height of the Cold War, and is remembered as one of Kennedy's finest and most important speeches.\\nSoviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev liked this speech so much that he ordered its full Russian translation published in Pravda and Izvestia, which was unprecendented at that time.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American%20University%20speech\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"American University\",\"American University Museum\",\"American University speech\"],\"depth\":3,\"links\":[\"1946 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1948 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1950 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1952 United States Senate election in Massachusetts\",\"1956 Democratic National Convention\",\"1958 C-130 shootdown incident\",\"1958 United States Senate election in Massachusetts\",\"1960 Democratic National Convention\",\"1960 Democratic Party presidential primaries\",\"1960 Democratic Party vice presidential candidate selection\",\"1960 RB-47 shootdown incident\",\"1960 U-2 incident\",\"1960 United States presidential debates\",\"1960 United States presidential election\",\"1961 F-84 Thunderstreak incident\",\"1962 State of the Union Address\",\"1963 State of the Union Address\",\"1964 T-39 shootdown incident\",\"1972 Olympic men's basketball final\",\"1973 United States–Soviet Union wheat deal\",\"1976 Philadelphia Flyers–Red Army game\",\"1980 Summer Olympics boycott\",\"1983 Soviet nuclear false alarm incident\",\"1984 Summer Olympics boycott\",\"1986 Black Sea incident\",\"19