ngton, D.C.\",\"Russian Empire–United States relations\",\"Russian Famine Relief Act\",\"Russian Life\",\"Russian Soviet Government Bureau\",\"Russian War Relief\",\"Russian ambassador's residence in Washington, D.C.\",\"Russia–United States relations\",\"S/Y Manitou\",\"SS-100-X\",\"SS Dakotan\",\"SS Indigirka\",\"SS Iowan\",\"SS Metallurg Anosov\",\"START I\",\"Samantha Smith\",\"Sardar (horse)\",\"Seaboard World Airlines Flight 253A\",\"Self-propelled barge T-36\",\"Seven Days to the River Rhine\",\"Sheldon names\",\"Shoe-banging incident\",\"Shostakovich v. Twentieth Century-Fox Film Corp.\",\"Shvetsov M-25\",\"Siberian Seven\",\"Siberian intervention\",\"Sisson Documents\",\"Situation Room\",\"Smolensk Archive\",\"Society for Technical Aid to Soviet Russia\",\"Sovereignty of Puerto Rico during the Cold War\",\"Sovfoto\",\"Soviet Government Purchasing Commission in the U.S.\",\"Soviet Interview Project\",\"Soviet Military Power\",\"Soviet Nuclear Threat Reduction Act of 1991\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Soviet Union–United States relations\",\"Soviet espionage in the United States\",\"Soviet submarine K-129 (1960)\",\"Soviet submarine K-219\",\"Soviet–American Gallium Experiment\",\"Space Race\",\"Space policy of the United States\",\"Spaso House\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Stalin Note\",\"Stalingrad (1990 film)\",\"Stand in the Schoolhouse Door\",\"State funeral of John F. Kennedy\",\"State of the Union\",\"State visit by Nikita Khrushchev to the United States\",\"Statue of John F. Kennedy (Boston)\",\"Stimson Doctrine\",\"Strategic Arms Limitation Talks\",\"Strategic Defense Initiative\",\"Student Struggle for Soviet Jewry\",\"Super Series\",\"Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program\",\"Suzanne Massie\",\"TOPAZ nuclear reactor\",\"Tatiana Schlossberg\",\"Tear down this wall!\",\"Ted Kennedy\",\"Ted Sorensen\",\"Tehran Conference\",\"The Admiral's Daughter\",\"The Extraordinary Adventures of Mr. West in the Land of the Bolsheviks\",\"The Moscow rules\",\"Threshold Test Ban Treaty\",\"Timeline of the Cold War\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"American University speech\"},\"ladderLevel\":0.13657003872925572,\"innerBudgetRemaining\":99.50000000000003,\"probeResult\":{\"pass\":true,\"results\":[]},\"isStable\":false,\"action\":{\"type\":\"NAVIGATE\",\"title\":\"Alliance for Progress\"},\"next\":{\"currentTitle\":\"Alliance for Progress\",\"summary\":\"The Alliance for Progress (Spanish: Alianza para el progreso) was an initiative launched by U.S. President John F. Kennedy on March 13, 1961, that aimed to establish economic cooperation between the U.S. and Latin America. Governor Luis Muñoz Marín of Puerto Rico was a close advisor on Latin American affairs to Kennedy, and one of his top administrators, Teodoro Moscoso, the architect of \\\"Operation Bootstrap\\\", was named the coordinator of the program by President Kennedy.\\nThe Alliance for Progress was a 10-year plan proposed by President John F. Kennedy in 1961 to foster economic cooperation between North and South America, particularly aimed at countering the perceived communist threat from Cuba. The program was signed at an inter-American conference in Uruguay in August 1961. The main objectives of the Alliance for Progress included:\\n\\nEconomic Development: The plan aimed to achieve an annual increase of 2.5 percent in per capita income in Latin American countries, with the goal of promoting economic growth and reducing poverty.\\nDemocratic Governments: The charter called for the establishment and support of democratic governments in Latin America, promoting political stability and the protection of human rights.\\nEducation and Literacy: One of the goals was to eliminate adult illiteracy in Latin America by 1970, recognizing education as a crucial factor for social and economic development.\\nPrice Stability: The Alliance aimed to maintain price stability in the region, avoiding high inflation or deflation, which could hinder economic progress.\\nIncome Distribution and Land Reform: The program sought to promote more equitable income distribution and land reform to address social and economic inequalities.\\nEconomic and Social Planning: Latin American countries were expected to create comprehensive plans for national development, which would be reviewed and approved by an inter-American board of experts.\\nFinancial Commitment: The participating Latin American countries pledged a capital investment of $80 billion over 10 years, while the United States would provide or guarantee $20 billion in aid.\\nTax Reform: The tax codes in Latin American countries were to be revised to demand a greater contribution from the wealthier individuals and corporations, aiming for a fairer distribution of the tax burden.\\nThe Alliance for Progress aimed to strengthen ties between the United States and Latin America, promoting economic growth, political stability, and social progress. However, the success of the program was limited due to various challenges, including political instability, corruption, and insufficient implementation of the proposed reforms.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alliance%20for%20Progress\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"American University\",\"American University Museum\",\"American University speech\",\"Alliance for Progress\"],\"depth\":4,\"links\":[\"1946 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1948 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1950 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1952 United States Senate election in Massachusetts\",\"1956 Democratic National Convention\",\"1958 United States Senate election in Massachusetts\",\"1960 Democratic National Convention\",\"1960 Democratic Party presidential primaries\",\"1960 United States presidential debates\",\"1960 United States presidential election\",\"1962 State of the Union Address\",\"1963 State of the Union Address\",\"A. J. Langguth\",\"A Nation of Immigrants\",\"A rising tide lifts all boats