n Act of 1991\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Soviet Union–United States relations\",\"Soviet espionage in the United States\",\"Soviet submarine K-129 (1960)\",\"Soviet submarine K-219\",\"Soviet–American Gallium Experiment\",\"Space Race\",\"Space policy of the United States\",\"Spaso House\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Stalin Note\",\"Stalingrad (1990 film)\",\"Stand in the Schoolhouse Door\",\"State funeral of John F. Kennedy\",\"State of the Union\",\"State visit by Nikita Khrushchev to the United States\",\"Statue of John F. Kennedy (Boston)\",\"Stimson Doctrine\",\"Strategic Arms Limitation Talks\",\"Strategic Defense Initiative\",\"Student Struggle for Soviet Jewry\",\"Super Series\",\"Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program\",\"Suzanne Massie\",\"TOPAZ nuclear reactor\",\"Tatiana Schlossberg\",\"Tear down this wall!\",\"Ted Kennedy\",\"Ted Sorensen\",\"Tehran Conference\",\"The Admiral's Daughter\",\"The Extraordinary Adventures of Mr. West in the Land of the Bolsheviks\",\"The Moscow rules\",\"Threshold Test Ban Treaty\",\"Timeline of the Cold War\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"American University speech\"},\"feedback\":-0.028111165678421923},{\"t\":5,\"state\":{\"currentTitle\":\"American University speech\",\"summary\":\"The American University speech, titled \\\"A Strategy of Peace\\\", was a commencement address delivered by United States President John F. Kennedy at the American University in Washington, D.C., on Monday, June 10, 1963. Widely considered one of the most powerful speeches Kennedy delivered, he not only outlined a plan to curb nuclear arms, but also \\\"laid out a hopeful, yet realistic route for world peace at a time when the U.S. and Soviet Union faced the potential for an escalating nuclear arms race.\\\" In the speech, Kennedy announced his agreement to negotiations \\\"toward early agreement on a comprehensive test ban treaty\\\" (which resulted in the Nuclear Test-Ban Treaty) and also announced, for the purpose of showing \\\"good faith and solemn convictions\\\", his decision to unilaterally suspend all U.S. atmospheric testing of nuclear weapons as long as all other nations would do the same. Noteworthy are his comments that the United States was seeking a goal of \\\"complete disarmament\\\" of nuclear weapons and his vow that America \\\"will never start a war\\\". \\nThe speech was unusual in its peaceful outreach to the Soviet Union at the height of the Cold War, and is remembered as one of Kennedy's finest and most important speeches.\\nSoviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev liked this speech so much that he ordered its full Russian translation published in Pravda and Izvestia, which was unprecendented at that time.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American%20University%20speech\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"American University\",\"American University Museum\",\"American University speech\",\"Alliance for Progress\",\"American University speech\"],\"depth\":5,\"links\":[\"1946 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1948 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1950 United States House of Representatives elections\",\"1952 United States Senate election in Massachusetts\",\"1956 Democratic National Convention\",\"1958 C-130 shootdown incident\",\"1958 United States Senate election in Massachusetts\",\"1960 Democratic National Convention\",\"1960 Democratic Party presidential primaries\",\"1960 Democratic Party vice presidential candidate selection\",\"1960 RB-47 shootdown incident\",\"1960 U-2 incident\",\"1960 United States presidential debates\",\"1960 United States presidential election\",\"1961 F-84 Thunderstreak incident\",\"1962 State of the Union Address\",\"1963 State of the Union Address\",\"1964 T-39 shootdown incident\",\"1972 Olympic men's basketball final\",\"1973 United States–Soviet Union wheat deal\",\"1976 Philadelphia Flyers–Red Army game\",\"1980 Summer Olympics boycott\",\"1983 Soviet nuclear false alarm incident\",\"1984 Summ