residents of the United States on U.S. postage stamps\",\"Profile in Courage Award\",\"Profiles in Courage\",\"Pushinka\",\"Reactions to the assassination of John F. Kennedy\",\"Remarks at Amherst College on the Arts\",\"Report to the American People on Civil Rights\",\"Resolute desk\",\"Response to the State of the Union address\",\"Revenue Act of 1962\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Robert F. Kennedy\",\"Ronald Reagan\",\"Roosevelt Corollary\",\"Rose Kennedy\",\"Rose Schlossberg\",\"Rosemary Kennedy\",\"Runnymede\",\"Rutherford B. Hayes\",\"S/Y Manitou\",\"SS-100-X\",\"Sardar (horse)\",\"Second Bill of Rights\",\"Situation Room\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Space policy of the United States\",\"Speaker of the United States House of Representatives\",\"Stand in the Schoolhouse Door\",\"State funeral of John F. Kennedy\",\"State of the City address\",\"State of the State address\",\"State of the Union\",\"State of the Union Address\",\"State of the Union address\",\"Statue of John F. Kennedy (Boston)\",\"Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program\",\"Tatiana Schlossberg\",\"Ted Kennedy\",\"Theodore Roosevelt\",\"Thomas Jefferson\",\"Timeline of the John F. Kennedy assassination\",\"Timeline of the John F. Kennedy presidency\",\"Trade Expansion Act\",\"Twenty-fourth Amendment to the United States Constitution\",\"USS John F. Kennedy (CV-67)\",\"USS John F. Kennedy (CVN-79)\",\"Ulysses S. Grant\",\"United States Agency for International Development\",\"United States Capitol\",\"United States Department of Housing and Urban Development\",\"United States Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\",\"United States House of Representatives\",\"Vice President of the United States\",\"Vienna summit\",\"Vietnam War\",\"Voter Education Project\",\"War on poverty\",\"Warren G. Harding\",\"Washington, D.C.\",\"We choose to go to the Moon\",\"Wetlands Loan Act\",\"Wexford (Marshall, Virginia)\",\"Why England Slept\",\"Wikisource\",\"William Henry Harrison\",\"William Howard Taft\",\"William McKinley\",\"Woodrow Wilson\",\"Yad Kennedy\",\"Zachary Taylor\",\"Template talk:John F. Kennedy\",\"Template talk:State of the Union\",\"Template talk:US-poli-stub\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"1963 State of the Union Address\"},\"feedback\":-0.16399141707444304},{\"t\":6,\"state\":{\"currentTitle\":\"1963 State of the Union Address\",\"summary\":\"The 1963 State of the Union Address was given by John F. Kennedy, the 35th president of the United States, on Monday, January 14, 1963, to the 88th United States Congress in the chamber of the United States House of Representatives. It was Kennedy's third and final State of the Union Address. Presiding over this joint session was House speaker John W. McCormack, accompanied by Vice President Lyndon B. Johnson, in his capacity as the president of the Senate.\\nKennedy's speech addressed various economic concerns, such as taxation policy. Kennedy called for a major revision of the federal taxation system, saying that \\\"To achieve these greater gains, one step, above all, is essential—the enactment this year of a substantial reduction and revision in Federal income taxes.\\\" Kennedy's speech also spent a significant time addressing foreign policy with regard to the Soviet Union and other Cold War-era concerns. He said that the United States must be prepared with a strong national defense, including nuclear weapons:\\n\\nFinally, what can we do to move from the present pause toward enduring peace? Again I would counsel caution. I foresee no spectacular reversal in Communist methods or goals. But if all these trends and developments can persuade the Soviet Union to walk the path of peace, then let her know that all free nations will journey with her. But until that choice is made, and until the world can develop a reliable system of international security, the free peoples have no choice but to keep their arms nearby. This country, therefore, continues to require the best defense in the world—a defense which is suited to the sixties. This means, unfortunately, a rising defense budget—for there is no substitute for adequate defense, and no \\\"bargain basement\\\" way of achieving it. It means the expenditure of more than $15 billion this year on nuclear weapons systems alone, a sum which is about equal to the combined defense budgets of our European Allies.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1963%20State%20of%20the%20Union%20Address\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"American University\",\"American University Museum\",\"American University speech\",\"Alliance for Progress\",\"American University speech\",\"1963 State of the Union Address\"],\"depth\":6,\"links\":[\"1791 State of the Union Address\",\"1792 State of the Union Address\",\"1793 State of the Union Address\",\"1794 State of the Union Address\",\"1795 State of the Union Address\",\"1796 State of the Union Address\",\"1797 State of the Union Address\",\"1798 State of the Union Address\",\"1799 State of the Union Address\",\"1800 State of the Union Address\",\"1801 State of the Union Address\",\"1802 State of the Union Address\",\"1803 State of the Union Address\",\"1804 State of the Union Address\",\"1805 State of the Union Address\",\"1806 State of the Union Address\",\"1807 State of the Union Address\",\"1808 State of the Union Address\",\"1809 State of the Union Address\",\"1810 State of the Union Address\",\"1811 State of the Union Address\",\"1812 State of the Union Address\",\"1813 State of the Union Address\",\"1814 State of the Union Address\",\"1815 State of the Union Address\",\"1816 State of the Union Address\",\"1817 State of the Union Address\",\"1818 State of the Union Address\",\"1819 State of the Union Address\",\"1820 State of the Union Address\",\"1821 State of the Union Address\",\"1822 State of the Union Address\",\"1823 State of the Union Address\",\"1824 State of the Union Address\",\"1825 State of the Union Address\",\"1826 State of the Union Address\",\"1827 State of the Union 